  "enhanced_information": "In gluconeogenesis, specific enzymes are required for certain reactions that differ from those in glycolysis. The reaction of Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate to Fructose-6-phosphate utilizes the enzyme Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, which is distinct from the enzyme used in glycolysis (phosphofructokinase). Therefore, this reaction requires a different enzyme. In contrast, the reactions converting Phosphoenolpyruvate to 2-phosphoglycerate, 2-phosphoglycerate to 3-phosphoglycerate, Dihydroxyacetone phosphate to Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate, and 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate all utilize enzymes that are the same as those in glycolysis. Thus, the only reaction that requires a different enzyme in gluconeogenesis is Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate to Fructose-6-phosphate."
